-- title:
--   Canonical L-map over W(k)/p, k algebraically closed
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, let $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{p^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ be a ring homomorphism, and write $B = W(k)/(p)$ for the quotient of the Witt vectors by the ideal generated by $p$, with $j$ the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map. Let $\Phi$ be a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $B$, that is, a $2$-dimensional commutative formal group law $\Phi.F$ over $B$ together with an action of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ by endomorphism series, additive and multiplicative in the parameter, and a series $\varpi$ with $\varpi\circ\varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi\circ[a] = [\sigma a]\circ\varpi$. Assume $\Phi$ is special for $j$: the two Lie eigenpieces $\mathrm{lieZero}\,j$ and $\mathrm{lieOne}\,j$ are complementary in $\mathrm{Lie}\,\Phi$ and each is an invertible $B$-module. Assume further that the graded pieces of index $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module $M = \mathrm{CartierModule}\,p\,\Phi.F$ — the subgroups on which each Teichmüller element $[c]$, $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$, acts by the homothety $j([c])^{p^n}$ — are complementary. Then the associated graded Cartier module datum $D = \Phi.\mathrm{toGradedCartierModuleData}\,j$, with underlying module $M$, operators $F$, $V$ and $\varpi$ and grading by these two pieces, admits an additive map $L : M \to D.\mathrm{NMod} = (M \times D.\mathrm{Sigma})/D.\mathrm{nRel}$ which is a canonical $L$-map: it is a Cartier $L$-map, and there are a commutative ring $S$ with a map $j_S$ from $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$, a surjection $\varphi : S \to B$ with $S$ having no $p$-torsion, a graded Cartier module datum $D_\ell$ over $S$ which is a special Cartier module (it possesses a homogeneous $V$-basis and is $V$-adically complete), a base-change map $f : D_\ell.M \to M$ along $\varphi$ and a Cartier $L$-map $L_\ell$ on $D_\ell$, such that $L \circ f$ agrees with the induced map of $N$-modules applied to $L_\ell$.
--
--   This supplies, at a geometric point, the hypothesis "a canonical $L$-map exists" used in Boutot–Carayol's construction of the Cartier-theoretic data attached to a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module in the proof of Drinfeld's classification theorem. It is the form of that hypothesis needed when $\Phi$ is given only as a special formal $\mathcal O_D$-module over $W(k)/p$ with $k$ algebraically closed, and it feeds the existence statements for Cartier quadruples of admissible rigidified formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules.
